WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:02.415
Lesson zero point six.

00:00:02.415 --> 00:00:05.070
Networks, gas, and your first transfer.

00:00:05.070 --> 00:00:07.326
By the end, you'll move crypto from the

00:00:07.326 --> 00:00:09.750
exchange to your own wallet safely, on the

00:00:09.750 --> 00:00:12.175
right network, starting with a test amount.

00:00:13.475 --> 00:00:16.070
Here's why this lesson is stricter than most.

00:00:16.070 --> 00:00:18.125
A wrong password can be reset.

00:00:18.125 --> 00:00:20.290
A wrong purchase can be sold back.

00:00:20.290 --> 00:00:23.063
But a transfer sent on the wrong network has

00:00:23.063 --> 00:00:26.083
no undo button, no support ticket that fixes it,

00:00:26.083 --> 00:00:28.725
and often no way to recover it at all.

00:00:28.725 --> 00:00:31.475
Everything in this lesson exists to make sure

00:00:31.475 --> 00:00:34.045
that specific mistake never happens to you.

00:00:35.345 --> 00:00:36.635
Here's the plan.

00:00:36.635 --> 00:00:38.861
First, networks, and why the same coin

00:00:38.861 --> 00:00:40.760
can live on several of them.

00:00:40.760 --> 00:00:44.355
Second, the golden rule that prevents the costly mistake.

00:00:44.355 --> 00:00:47.120
Third, the six-step transfer, done safely.

00:00:47.120 --> 00:00:49.584
And finally, a real worked example with

00:00:49.584 --> 00:00:52.048
real numbers, your checklist and a quiz.

00:00:53.348 --> 00:00:54.123
Networks.

00:00:55.423 --> 00:00:58.888
Quick context on why several networks exist at all.

00:00:58.888 --> 00:01:03.073
Ethereum alone can only process so many transactions per

00:01:03.073 --> 00:01:07.406
second, and when demand outstrips that, fees rise sharply,

00:01:07.406 --> 00:01:10.863
exactly what Lesson 0.1's gas chart showed.

00:01:10.863 --> 00:01:13.479
Layer 2s exist specifically to relieve that

00:01:13.479 --> 00:01:16.274
pressure, moving most activity off the busiest

00:01:16.274 --> 00:01:18.653
network while still settling back to it.

00:01:19.953 --> 00:01:22.421
The same token, USDC for example,

00:01:22.421 --> 00:01:25.842
can exist on several different blockchains.

00:01:25.842 --> 00:01:27.752
Ethereum is the main one: the

00:01:27.752 --> 00:01:30.281
deepest security, the highest fees.

00:01:30.281 --> 00:01:33.812
Layer 2 networks, like Arbitrum, Base and Optimism,

00:01:33.812 --> 00:01:37.208
are built around Ethereum, inheriting much of its

00:01:37.208 --> 00:01:39.653
security, at a fraction of the cost.

00:01:40.953 --> 00:01:43.143
Here's a detail worth knowing early.

00:01:43.143 --> 00:01:46.769
Your wallet address is usually the same across Ethereum

00:01:46.769 --> 00:01:50.048
and its Layer 2s, one address, several chains.

00:01:50.048 --> 00:01:52.603
But your balances are separate on each one.

00:01:52.603 --> 00:01:54.738
Switch networks inside your wallet to

00:01:54.738 --> 00:01:56.648
see what's actually there on each.

00:01:57.948 --> 00:02:00.017
And here's roughly why a Layer 2 can

00:02:00.017 --> 00:02:02.778
be so much cheaper while still being secure.

00:02:02.778 --> 00:02:05.958
It bundles up many transactions together, off the

00:02:05.958 --> 00:02:09.138
main Ethereum network, then posts a compact proof

00:02:09.138 --> 00:02:11.918
of all of them back to Ethereum itself.

00:02:11.918 --> 00:02:14.823
You still get Ethereum's security backing it up.

00:02:14.823 --> 00:02:17.154
You just aren't paying for space on the

00:02:17.154 --> 00:02:20.708
busiest, most expensive network for every single transaction.

00:02:22.008 --> 00:02:25.320
One practical habit: always check which network you're

00:02:25.320 --> 00:02:27.908
actually on before you act, not after.

00:02:27.908 --> 00:02:29.965
Most wallets show a network name or

00:02:29.965 --> 00:02:32.093
logo near the top of the screen.

00:02:32.093 --> 00:02:34.694
Look at it before every withdrawal, every

00:02:34.694 --> 00:02:37.728
swap, every transfer, until it becomes automatic.

00:02:39.028 --> 00:02:40.013
The golden rule.

00:02:41.313 --> 00:02:43.783
Here it is, in one sentence, because this

00:02:43.783 --> 00:02:46.611
is the one rule this whole lesson protects.

00:02:46.611 --> 00:02:49.528
The network you withdraw on must match a network

00:02:49.528 --> 00:02:53.221
your wallet, or the receiving service, actually supports.

00:02:53.221 --> 00:02:55.098
Sending on the wrong network can

00:02:55.098 --> 00:02:56.861
mean the funds are simply lost.

00:02:58.161 --> 00:03:00.766
Here's exactly how that mistake happens.

00:03:00.766 --> 00:03:03.615
You withdraw from the exchange, and pick a network

00:03:03.615 --> 00:03:06.876
without really checking it, maybe the default option.

00:03:06.876 --> 00:03:09.067
Your wallet, or the receiving service,

00:03:09.067 --> 00:03:11.501
doesn't actually support that network.

00:03:11.501 --> 00:03:14.794
The transaction still goes through, because the blockchain

00:03:14.794 --> 00:03:16.936
has no idea what you meant to do.

00:03:16.936 --> 00:03:19.052
And the funds land somewhere you can't

00:03:19.052 --> 00:03:20.896
see or reach, often gone for good.

00:03:22.196 --> 00:03:24.151
Notice there's no villain in that

00:03:24.151 --> 00:03:26.464
chain, no hacker, no phishing site.

00:03:26.464 --> 00:03:28.061
Just a dropdown menu, and a

00:03:28.061 --> 00:03:29.844
moment of not checking it.

00:03:29.844 --> 00:03:32.554
That's exactly why the fix isn't cleverness.

00:03:32.554 --> 00:03:34.596
It's a habit, the same small check,

00:03:34.596 --> 00:03:36.694
done the same way, every single time.

00:03:37.994 --> 00:03:40.400
Here are the shapes this mistake actually

00:03:40.400 --> 00:03:42.819
takes, so you recognise them by name.

00:03:42.819 --> 00:03:45.721
Withdrawing to an exchange deposit address on

00:03:45.721 --> 00:03:48.370
the wrong network, when the exchange only

00:03:48.370 --> 00:03:50.815
credits deposits on specific ones.

00:03:50.815 --> 00:03:52.467
Sending to a wallet that simply

00:03:52.467 --> 00:03:54.780
doesn't support the network you picked.

00:03:54.780 --> 00:03:57.700
And assuming an address looks the same everywhere,

00:03:57.700 --> 00:04:00.277
so it must work everywhere, which is exactly

00:04:00.277 --> 00:04:02.510
the assumption that catches people out.

00:04:03.810 --> 00:04:04.900
Quick check.

00:04:04.900 --> 00:04:09.505
You hold USDC on Arbitrum, but zero ETH there.

00:04:09.505 --> 00:04:10.418
Can you send the USDC?

00:04:10.418 --> 00:04:11.490
[[pause 4]] The answer: no.

00:04:15.910 --> 00:04:18.105
You need a little ETH on

00:04:18.105 --> 00:04:21.830
Arbitrum specifically to pay gas there.

00:04:21.830 --> 00:04:24.685
ETH on Ethereum mainnet doesn't help.

00:04:25.985 --> 00:04:27.220
Your first transfer.

00:04:28.520 --> 00:04:31.179
Here's the whole transfer in one picture, before

00:04:31.179 --> 00:04:33.215
we walk through it step by step.

00:04:33.215 --> 00:04:36.285
Copy, choose, check, test, wait, then send the rest.

00:04:37.585 --> 00:04:38.980
Steps one and two.

00:04:38.980 --> 00:04:41.521
In your wallet, copy your address, using the

00:04:41.521 --> 00:04:43.910
copy button, never typing it by hand.

00:04:43.910 --> 00:04:47.329
On the exchange, choose withdraw, then the coin, then

00:04:47.329 --> 00:04:50.494
pick the network: for learning, a low-fee Layer 2

00:04:50.494 --> 00:04:53.849
your wallet actually supports, like Arbitrum or Base.

00:04:55.149 --> 00:04:56.994
Steps three and four, in full.

00:04:56.994 --> 00:04:59.090
Paste the address into the withdrawal

00:04:59.090 --> 00:05:00.989
field, never type it by hand.

00:05:00.989 --> 00:05:03.839
Check the first 6 characters match your wallet.

00:05:03.839 --> 00:05:06.009
Check the last 6 characters too.

00:05:06.009 --> 00:05:08.818
Better still, check the whole address, character

00:05:08.818 --> 00:05:11.168
by character, since a single wrong digit

00:05:11.168 --> 00:05:13.359
sends it somewhere else entirely.

00:05:13.359 --> 00:05:16.652
Then choose an amount for a small test first,

00:05:16.652 --> 00:05:20.661
something like $10 of ETH, before anything larger moves.

00:05:21.961 --> 00:05:23.586
Steps five and six.

00:05:23.586 --> 00:05:26.206
Wait for the test to arrive, and confirm you

00:05:26.206 --> 00:05:29.126
see it in your wallet, on that exact network.

00:05:29.126 --> 00:05:30.951
Only then, send the rest.

00:05:30.951 --> 00:05:34.502
And add the address to the exchange's withdrawal allowlist,

00:05:34.502 --> 00:05:37.816
so future transfers to your own wallet are pre-approved.

00:05:39.116 --> 00:05:40.993
If the test doesn't show up within a

00:05:40.993 --> 00:05:43.576
few minutes, don't panic and don't send more.

00:05:43.576 --> 00:05:45.592
Check you're actually looking at the right

00:05:45.592 --> 00:05:47.467
network in your wallet, since it's easy

00:05:47.467 --> 00:05:49.126
to be watching the wrong one.

00:05:49.126 --> 00:05:52.807
Check the transaction on that network's block explorer,

00:05:52.807 --> 00:05:55.933
using the transaction ID from the exchange.

00:05:55.933 --> 00:05:58.380
And if the network is simply congested, a

00:05:58.380 --> 00:06:00.593
longer wait is often the whole answer.

00:06:01.893 --> 00:06:04.378
How small should that test actually be?

00:06:04.378 --> 00:06:08.210
Small enough that losing it entirely wouldn't bother you,

00:06:08.210 --> 00:06:11.316
the same principle Lesson 0.0 used for a whole

00:06:11.316 --> 00:06:15.118
learning budget, just applied to one single transfer.

00:06:15.118 --> 00:06:17.569
$10 is a reasonable starting point for

00:06:17.569 --> 00:06:20.147
most people; scale it to whatever number

00:06:20.147 --> 00:06:22.158
actually meets that bar for you.

00:06:23.458 --> 00:06:24.548
Quick check.

00:06:24.548 --> 00:06:27.043
Why send a small test amount before the rest?

00:06:31.463 --> 00:06:33.724
The answer: to confirm the address and

00:06:33.724 --> 00:06:36.216
the network are actually right, while only

00:06:36.216 --> 00:06:38.023
a small amount is at risk.

00:06:38.023 --> 00:06:40.228
If something's wrong, you find out cheaply.

00:06:41.528 --> 00:06:42.933
A real worked example.

00:06:44.233 --> 00:06:48.947
Withdrawing $100 of ETH to Arbitrum typically costs the

00:06:48.947 --> 00:06:54.213
exchange somewhere between $0.10 and $1 in withdrawal fees.

00:06:54.213 --> 00:06:57.153
Small, one-time, and worth it for what it unlocks.

00:06:58.453 --> 00:07:00.128
Here's what that unlocks.

00:07:00.128 --> 00:07:02.649
A swap on Arbitrum afterwards typically

00:07:02.649 --> 00:07:04.524
costs a few cents in gas.

00:07:04.524 --> 00:07:07.414
The same swap on Ethereum mainnet might cost a

00:07:07.414 --> 00:07:10.359
few dollars, more when the network is busy.

00:07:10.359 --> 00:07:13.106
That gap, cents against dollars, is exactly

00:07:13.106 --> 00:07:15.103
why beginners learn on Layer 2s.

00:07:16.403 --> 00:07:18.289
That's the whole reason this program

00:07:18.289 --> 00:07:19.813
starts you on a Layer 2.

00:07:19.813 --> 00:07:22.543
It's not because Ethereum mainnet is wrong.

00:07:22.543 --> 00:07:24.953
It's because learning costs real money there.

00:07:24.953 --> 00:07:27.199
Cents instead of dollars means you can

00:07:27.199 --> 00:07:29.388
make a mistake, or just try something

00:07:29.388 --> 00:07:32.153
twice, without it costing anything that matters.

00:07:33.453 --> 00:07:37.213
That doesn't make Ethereum mainnet something to avoid forever.

00:07:37.213 --> 00:07:40.261
As your habits solidify and the amounts involved

00:07:40.261 --> 00:07:43.559
grow, some activity genuinely belongs there, for its

00:07:43.559 --> 00:07:46.658
deeper liquidity and its longer track record.

00:07:46.658 --> 00:07:49.063
Learn the habits small and cheap, first.

00:07:49.063 --> 00:07:50.749
Then decide where to apply

00:07:50.749 --> 00:07:52.998
them, deliberately, not by accident.

00:07:54.298 --> 00:07:55.478
Checklist and quiz.

00:07:56.778 --> 00:07:58.673
Here's this lesson's checklist.

00:07:58.673 --> 00:08:02.288
Network chosen on purpose, and supported by your wallet.

00:08:02.288 --> 00:08:05.763
Address pasted, first and last 6 characters checked.

00:08:05.763 --> 00:08:08.858
A test amount sent and received before the rest.

00:08:08.858 --> 00:08:12.303
A little ETH held on that network for gas.

00:08:12.303 --> 00:08:15.528
And the address added to the exchange's withdrawal allowlist.

00:08:16.827 --> 00:08:18.097
Question three.

00:08:18.097 --> 00:08:21.069
What's the golden rule of withdrawals, in one sentence?

00:08:25.489 --> 00:08:28.257
The answer: the network you send on must be

00:08:28.257 --> 00:08:31.654
one the receiving wallet or service actually supports.

00:08:32.954 --> 00:08:34.209
Question four.

00:08:34.209 --> 00:08:37.792
True or false: your wallet address changes when you

00:08:37.792 --> 00:08:41.099
switch from Ethereum to a Layer 2 like Arbitrum.

00:08:45.399 --> 00:08:47.034
The answer: false.

00:08:47.034 --> 00:08:49.249
The address usually stays the same.

00:08:49.249 --> 00:08:51.679
It's the balance on each network that's separate.

00:08:52.979 --> 00:08:54.294
Question five.

00:08:54.294 --> 00:08:56.122
Your test transfer hasn't shown

00:08:56.122 --> 00:08:57.736
up after a few minutes.

00:08:57.736 --> 00:08:59.211
What's the first thing to check?

00:09:03.631 --> 00:09:05.592
The answer: that you're actually viewing

00:09:05.592 --> 00:09:07.566
the correct network in your wallet.

00:09:07.566 --> 00:09:09.434
It's easy to be watching the wrong

00:09:09.434 --> 00:09:11.516
one and assume the transfer is missing.

00:09:12.816 --> 00:09:15.626
Here's the whole lesson, recapped as one loop.

00:09:15.626 --> 00:09:16.976
Copy your address.

00:09:16.976 --> 00:09:19.726
Choose a network your wallet actually supports.

00:09:19.726 --> 00:09:22.476
Check the address, first and last characters.

00:09:22.476 --> 00:09:24.171
Send a small test first.

00:09:24.171 --> 00:09:25.661
Confirm it arrives.

00:09:25.661 --> 00:09:27.509
And only then, send the rest, and

00:09:27.509 --> 00:09:29.766
allowlist the address for next time.

00:09:29.766 --> 00:09:32.151
Do those six things, and this lesson is done.

00:09:33.451 --> 00:09:36.496
This is education, not financial advice.

00:09:36.496 --> 00:09:38.846
Take your time on this lesson especially.

00:09:38.846 --> 00:09:40.355
It's the one with the least

00:09:40.355 --> 00:09:42.056
room for a casual mistake.

00:09:42.056 --> 00:09:44.960
And nobody from this program will ever ask for

00:09:44.960 --> 00:09:47.987
your seed phrase, private keys or account access.

00:09:49.287 --> 00:09:52.112
That's networks, gas, and your first transfer.

00:09:52.112 --> 00:09:55.572
Copy, choose, check, test, confirm, then send the rest.

00:09:55.572 --> 00:09:58.153
Next up, Lesson zero point seven: your

00:09:58.153 --> 00:10:00.867
first DeFi steps, in practice mode first.
